ption {:value "925231"} "Individual, Professional, Men"]] [:script {} "$('#same_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#same_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:form {:class "form-inline form-inline-header"} [:select {:name "id", :id "other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "In other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "351037"} "2016"] "\n" [:option {:value "19001127"} "2020"] "\n" [:option {:value "2000907"} "2024"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/307"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Individual, Women" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} " 3 October 1900"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/199"} "Golf de Compiègne, Compiègne"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/40001"} "10 from 3 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "9 holes stroke play"]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The ladies’ event took place the day after the " [:a {:href "/results/40000"} "men’s event"] " and was won by " [:a {:href "/athletes/18138"} "Margaret Abbott"] " of the Chicago Golf Club, who played her requisite nine holes in 47 strokes. Behind Margaret Abbott in the women’s tournament came " [:a {:href "/athletes/18210"} "Polly Whittier"] ", and a lady listed in the past as Mrs. " [:a {:href "/athletes/18191"} "Thomas Huger Pratt"] ", of Dinard, France.  The three women were mysteries until research by Dr. Paula Welch, a sports historian at the University of Florida, unearthed their identities."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Margaret Abbott was born in Calcutta (Kolkata), India, in 1876, to wealthy parents.  She learned her golf at the Chicago Golf Club, but in 1900 was studying art in Paris, accompanied by " [:a {:href "/athletes/18139"} "her mother"] ", who also played in the Olympic golf tournament (she finished seventh).  By winning the Olympic golf tournament, she became the first American woman to win an Olympic event (and only the second overall)."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Polly Whittier, often listed as being from Switzerland, was actually Pauline Whittier, of Boston.  Also from a wealthy family, Whittier was a descendent of the famous poet John Greenleaf Whittier, and in 1900 was studying in St. Moritz, hence the mistaken affiliation."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Abbie, or more properly Mrs. Thomas Huger Pratt, of Dinard, was the former Daria Pankhurst.  Vacationing in France in 1900, she played her golf at the Dinard Club.  A short time after the Olympics, Daria Pratt’s second husband, Mr. Thomas Huger Pratt, died an early death.  She later married Prince Alexis Karageorgevitch of Serbia, thus becoming the Princess Karageorgevitch of Serbia."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Score"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/18138"} "Margaret Abbott"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "47"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/18210"} "Polly Whittier"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SUI"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SUI.png"}] "SUI"]] [:td {} "49"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/18191"} "Abbie Pratt"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amaz